    "id": "rates-intermediate-r5-1-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "The concentration $C$ of a drug in the bloodstream, in mg/L, is modelled by $C=Ae^{kt}$, where $t$ is the time in hours after the drug is given and $k=-0.3$.\n\nThe concentration is 18 mg/L after 4 hours.\n\nFind the rate at which the concentration is changing after 10 hours. Give your answer in mg/L per hour, correct to 3 decimal places.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$C'(10)\\approx-0.893\\text{ mg/L per hour}$.\n\nThe concentration is decreasing at $0.893\\text{ mg/L per hour}$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}C&=Ae^{-0.3t}\\\\18&=Ae^{-0.3(4)}\\\\18&=Ae^{-1.2}\\\\A&=18e^{1.2}\\\\C&=18e^{1.2}e^{-0.3t}\\end{align*}$$\nAt $t=10$:\n$$\\begin{align*}C(10)&=18e^{1.2}e^{-3}\\\\C(10)&=18e^{-1.8}\\\\\\frac{dC}{dt}&=kC\\\\C'(10)&=-0.3\\left(18e^{-1.8}\\right)\\\\C'(10)&\\approx-0.893\\text{ mg/L per hour}\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 37
  },
